# One process, many threads. Every flag here is load-bearing.
#
# --workers 1 because rooms live in memory (engine/rooms.py) and the bus that fans
# them out is a dict in this process. A second worker is a second copy of every room:
# create lands on one, say lands on the other, and the other answers 404 for a room it
# has never heard of. It is the same constraint as max-instances 1, one level down -
# workers are that problem inside a single instance.
#
# --threads because the default sync worker serves one request at a time, and a room
# holds a server-sent event stream open for as long as anybody is looking at it. With
# one thread the first viewer takes the only worker and the backend stops answering
# anyone - then thirty seconds later gunicorn's watchdog kills the worker for being
# busy and takes every room in memory with it.
#
# --timeout 0 for that watchdog: a streaming request is *meant* to sit there for hours,
# and the watchdog cannot tell that from a hang.
web: gunicorn -b :$PORT --workers 1 --threads 32 --timeout 0 server:app
